(snaps fingers)

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:10.041
- Shut up.

00:02:10.041 --> 00:02:11.581
- [Voiceover] Let's go.

00:02:11.581 --> 00:02:12.850
One more time.

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:20.099
- [Everyone in unison]
J-E-T-S, Jets, Jets, Jets.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:24.049
- Alright, India.

00:02:24.049 --> 00:02:24.689
- [India] Yeah.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:25.768
- You're a little rusty doing this.

00:02:25.768 --> 00:02:26.589
- I am, too.

00:02:26.589 --> 00:02:29.898
- Let's get into the show.

00:02:29.898 --> 00:02:31.302
- [Voiceover] Jordan asks,

00:02:31.302 --> 00:02:33.739
"I've been hustling since the age of 14."

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:35.999
"I now work 12, maybe 15 hours a day."

00:02:35.999 --> 00:02:38.569
"I love what I do, but
sometimes I fear my drive"

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:40.949
"works against me,
particularly when it comes to"

00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:43.150
"getting in the way of
relationships with friends."

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.370
"How do you deal with that?"

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.470
"I can't switch off."

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.749
- Yeah, I mean, this is something
I struggle with as well,

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:52.049
I mean when you're on 24/7-365,
it's difficult to shut

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:56.482
down, it's difficult to
change from in the zone to

00:02:56.482 --> 00:02:57.942
in the zone, I don't, and it

00:02:57.942 --> 00:03:00.002
works in reverse, too,
and I'm sure, right, like

00:03:00.002 --> 00:03:02.262
right now it's hard for
me to re-trigger back into

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:04.463
the work zone off the high

00:03:04.463 --> 00:03:07.302
of the sports brain
that I was rolling with

00:03:07.302 --> 00:03:08.463
over the weekend.

00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:09.962
Look, I think that

00:03:09.962 --> 00:03:12.442
actions speak louder than words.

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:14.343
And if these relationships matter to you,

00:03:14.343 --> 00:03:15.695
you're gonna figure out how to hack

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:16.498
and figure it out.

00:03:16.498 --> 00:03:18.658
It's as simple as that, there's
just nothing else to say.

00:03:18.658 --> 00:03:19.138
Hello.

00:03:19.138 --> 00:03:21.098
And so, you know,

00:03:21.098 --> 00:03:22.078
you've got to put in the work.

00:03:22.078 --> 00:03:23.678
You've got to learn how
to shut down, I mean

00:03:23.678 --> 00:03:25.078
sometimes it takes 
an hour, two hours,

00:03:25.078 --> 00:03:26.558
three hours, four hours.

00:03:26.558 --> 00:03:28.078
Maybe some sort of routine, you know

00:03:28.078 --> 00:03:29.619
working out has been a good transition

00:03:29.619 --> 00:03:31.258
period for me, you know.

00:03:31.258 --> 00:03:32.818
That's worked for me in the past,

00:03:32.818 --> 00:03:34.599
or in the recent times.

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:35.838
Something that lets you transition.

00:03:35.838 --> 00:03:37.498
Or what I tend to do...

00:03:37.498 --> 00:03:38.518
Hey, little man...

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:39.358
- Oh, sorry.

00:03:39.358 --> 00:03:40.899
- No, no worries.

00:03:40.899 --> 00:03:42.858
I think that another thing that I did

00:03:42.858 --> 00:03:44.278
that was kind of strategic was

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:47.138
I rated and ranked my relationships

00:03:47.138 --> 00:03:50.018
and then when I was segueing
into off of work mode

00:03:50.018 --> 00:03:51.738
into a weekend or the holidays,

00:03:51.738 --> 00:03:53.278
I would

00:03:53.278 --> 00:03:56.318
actually schedule meetings according to

00:03:56.318 --> 00:03:57.698
what I thought of them,

00:03:57.698 --> 00:03:59.478
so like, my mom would be later than maybe

00:03:59.478 --> 00:04:00.958
a solid friend.

00:04:00.958 --> 00:04:02.598
Because then I'd feel
like I was in the rhythm

00:04:02.598 --> 00:04:04.558
of that zone, so.

00:04:04.558 --> 00:04:06.539
I think you've gotta hack
and make it work for you,

00:04:06.539 --> 00:04:09.178
but your actions are louder
than your words, meaning

00:04:09.178 --> 00:04:12.158
you can't have the excuse
of, "My brain is always on."

00:04:12.158 --> 00:04:13.858
It means that you've chosen your business,

00:04:13.858 --> 00:04:15.918
your entrepreneurship and your ambition

00:04:15.918 --> 00:04:17.438
over those relationships.

00:04:17.438 --> 00:04:18.918
Which, by the way, and this is not

00:04:18.918 --> 00:04:20.558
super popular,

00:04:20.558 --> 00:04:22.338
I find that to be okay.

00:04:22.338 --> 00:04:24.178
I don't think it's noble.

00:04:24.178 --> 00:04:25.299
I don't think it's nice.

00:04:25.299 --> 00:04:26.418
It's just a reality.

00:04:26.418 --> 00:04:27.958
And there's a lot of people that do it.

00:04:27.958 --> 00:04:29.459
You know, especially as you look

00:04:29.459 --> 00:04:32.019
higher and higher up the pyramid.

00:04:32.019 --> 00:04:33.198
You look at the Oprahs

00:04:33.198 --> 00:04:34.518
and the Michael Jordans.

00:04:34.518 --> 00:04:35.218
You know.

00:04:35.218 --> 00:04:38.338
These are the choices
that people make, and so

00:04:38.338 --> 00:04:40.398
I think you've gotta make yours,

00:04:40.398 --> 00:04:42.438
and I think that you
need to live your life

00:04:42.438 --> 00:04:45.098
and not take the judgment
of all these people.

00:04:45.098 --> 00:04:46.778
However,

00:04:46.778 --> 00:04:49.018
you know, just accomplishing your goals,

00:04:49.018 --> 00:04:51.159
the monies, the sports teams,

00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:52.658
whatever they are,

00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:54.798
to have that by yourself,
and not to share that

00:04:54.798 --> 00:04:56.218
with the people you love the most,

00:04:56.218 --> 00:04:57.918
I think is a fairly lonely
place, and you need to

00:04:57.918 --> 00:05:00.428
keep that into serious consideration.

00:05:00.428 --> 00:05:02.018
- [Voiceover] Sahil asks,

00:05:02.018 --> 00:05:03.378
"Why did you stop forcing people to ask

00:05:03.378 --> 00:05:05.679
"questions from Instagram?"

00:05:05.679 --> 00:05:07.178
- This is a great
question, Sahil, I actually

00:05:07.178 --> 00:05:08.959
picked this out and sent it to the team.

00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:12.838
Because, I felt that I
finally had picked up

00:05:12.838 --> 00:05:14.918
my rhythm and momentum on Instagram.

00:05:14.918 --> 00:05:17.018
If you look back at the
point when I was really

00:05:17.018 --> 00:05:20.978
pushing that narrative as
a function of this show,

00:05:20.978 --> 00:05:24.558
I was hovering in the 30, 40,
50 thousand follower range.

00:05:24.558 --> 00:05:26.658
Not that following matters,
but don't forget, on Instagram

00:05:26.658 --> 00:05:29.358
it matters a little bit more,
because I feel the attention

00:05:29.358 --> 00:05:31.591
grab was there and
everybody's seeing everything,

00:05:31.591 --> 00:05:33.212
so I'd rather have a
hundred thousand there,

00:05:33.212 --> 00:05:35.211
than maybe a million person e-mail list or

00:05:35.211 --> 00:05:36.551
a million Twitter followers.

00:05:36.551 --> 00:05:37.971
So I was believing in Instagram.

00:05:37.971 --> 00:05:39.232
I knew I needed to build it up,

00:05:39.232 --> 00:05:42.071
and really, I created
a function on this show

00:05:42.071 --> 00:05:44.171
to create more awareness,

00:05:44.171 --> 00:05:45.231
more behavior,

00:05:45.231 --> 00:05:47.231
more interaction between me and you,

00:05:47.231 --> 00:05:49.491
and Instagram, and you and your friends

00:05:49.491 --> 00:05:51.831
on Instagram finding out
about me on Instagram.

00:05:51.831 --> 00:05:54.731
So it was very tactical, very strategic.

00:05:54.731 --> 00:05:57.871
I stopped asking because I feel as though

00:05:57.871 --> 00:05:59.951
I've gotten to a better place
where I've got better cadence

00:05:59.951 --> 00:06:02.091
and momentum, there's ads now.

00:06:02.091 --> 00:06:04.271
I don't want to create unlimited friction

00:06:04.271 --> 00:06:06.511
with you and myself so, like,

00:06:06.511 --> 00:06:07.751
I want you to be able to ask questions

00:06:07.751 --> 00:06:09.692
the way you want to ask them.

00:06:09.692 --> 00:06:12.111
So, it was a strategy in a moment of time

00:06:12.111 --> 00:06:14.531
for three to four months.

00:06:14.531 --> 00:06:16.611
I executed what I needed out of it,

00:06:16.611 --> 00:06:18.172
and I move on the the next
strategy, and the reason I

00:06:18.172 --> 00:06:20.051
picked this question, is I
want to make it very ever green

00:06:20.051 --> 00:06:22.711
to everybody and make them understand that

00:06:22.711 --> 00:06:24.751
strategies have life cycles.

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:26.212
What worked for you three, four years ago,

00:06:26.212 --> 00:06:28.291
or three, four months ago,
or three, four days ago,

00:06:28.291 --> 00:06:30.292
can be debated and switched.

00:06:30.292 --> 00:06:32.531
And I think way too many people
find something that works

00:06:32.531 --> 00:06:34.492
for them, and they milk it
until there's nothing left

00:06:34.492 --> 00:06:37.551
in the cow.

00:06:37.551 --> 00:06:38.791
- [Voiceover] Charles asks,

00:06:38.791 --> 00:06:41.101
"What service do you use to
make these amazing images?"

00:06:42.071 --> 00:06:43.611
- Charles, that's a
very, very good question.

00:06:43.611 --> 00:06:45.091
This one's super easy.

00:06:45.091 --> 00:06:47.071
This speaks to something
I believe, which is

00:06:47.071 --> 00:06:48.432
scaling the unscalable.

00:06:48.432 --> 00:06:50.571
Every time people ask
me about image quotes.

00:06:50.571 --> 00:06:51.851
This one (camera clicks),

00:06:51.851 --> 00:06:53.371
and this one (camera clicks).

00:06:53.371 --> 00:06:54.271
You got some work today, Staphon.

00:06:54.271 --> 00:06:55.691
And this one (camera clicks).

00:06:55.691 --> 00:06:56.891
People always tweet, and they're like,

00:06:56.891 --> 00:06:58.371
"What app do you use, what do you do?"

00:06:58.371 --> 00:06:59.051
Nope.

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:01.112
Just use the great Andrew
and Zak design team.

00:07:01.112 --> 00:07:03.751
Mainly Andrew now, who also
designed this amazing t-shirt.

00:07:03.751 --> 00:07:05.291
Buy it, now (bell rings).

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:08.171
And so, you know, really
what I'm doing there

00:07:08.171 --> 00:07:10.551
is just scaling the unscaleable.

00:07:10.551 --> 00:07:12.011
You know, there is no app.

00:07:12.011 --> 00:07:13.631
Everybody thinks everything's
got to be so scalable.

00:07:13.631 --> 00:07:14.911
No, this is done by hand.

00:07:14.911 --> 00:07:16.671
I have a design person, obviously look.

00:07:16.671 --> 00:07:17.772
And it's interesting.

00:07:17.772 --> 00:07:19.971
I have debated turning it into an app

00:07:19.971 --> 00:07:22.671
and opening it up to everybody.

00:07:22.671 --> 00:07:24.931
But for now it's just done by hand.

00:07:24.931 --> 00:07:26.951
There's a lot of texting.

00:07:26.951 --> 00:07:28.572
I'll actually pull out my phone, yeah.

00:07:28.572 --> 00:07:31.271
There's a lot of texting that
goes on between me and Andrew.

00:07:31.271 --> 00:07:32.931
Where I just get a thought

00:07:32.931 --> 00:07:35.071
and, you know, just.

00:07:35.071 --> 00:07:36.131
Get ready, DRock.

00:07:36.131 --> 00:07:38.391
Are you gonna wanna have my?

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:39.951
Are you gonna wanna have my phone?

00:07:39.951 --> 00:07:42.491
No, I'm not gonna give it to you, so...

00:07:42.491 --> 00:07:44.032
Because you're not gonna
be there, so you can't do

00:07:44.032 --> 00:07:45.651
it like you like to normally edit.

00:07:45.651 --> 00:07:49.131
But let's just find Andrew.

00:07:49.131 --> 00:07:51.231
And...

00:07:51.231 --> 00:07:52.721
Okay.

00:07:54.311 --> 00:07:54.871
- [DRock] Yeah.

00:07:54.871 --> 00:07:56.022
- See it?
- [DRock] Yeah.

00:07:56.022 --> 00:07:57.011
- So there you go.

00:07:57.011 --> 00:07:57.931
You could've been a bus.

00:07:57.931 --> 00:07:59.711
With an image of me from
the Monday morning video

00:07:59.711 --> 00:08:00.851
in the background.

00:08:00.851 --> 00:08:02.891
And a smaller font saying
"Watch the video now."

00:08:02.891 --> 00:08:05.451
I mean, you now, and

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:08.252
then it happens.

00:08:08.252 --> 00:08:09.911
Good, old-fashioned

00:08:09.911 --> 00:08:11.671
thoughts and execution.

00:08:11.671 --> 00:08:13.341
Keep it simple.

00:08:14.371 --> 00:08:15.611
Let's go.

00:08:15.611 --> 00:08:16.631
Where was I going with that?

00:08:16.631 --> 00:08:19.201
I feel like I was about to
go into some sort of like...

00:08:22.012 --> 00:08:23.491
- [Voicoever] Igor asks,

00:08:23.491 --> 00:08:26.671
"When is it okay to lie in business?"

00:08:26.671 --> 00:08:28.451
- That's interesting.

00:08:29.741 --> 00:08:32.390
The 14-year-old version
of me would have said,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:35.191
"Anytime, all the time, go for it."

00:08:35.191 --> 00:08:37.452
You know, I think the answer is never.

00:08:37.452 --> 00:08:39.671
I don't see any value prop.

00:08:39.671 --> 00:08:43.111
I think that having self-confidence and

00:08:43.111 --> 00:08:46.431
believing that you can do
something you've never done before

00:08:46.431 --> 00:08:48.591
is a great thing for business.

00:08:48.591 --> 00:08:50.132
But I think you gotta lay
those cards out to the

00:08:50.132 --> 00:08:51.751
person that's making that decision.

00:08:51.751 --> 00:08:53.912
You can't say, "We've executed
a campaign like this before,"

00:08:53.912 --> 00:08:54.711
Sorry.

00:08:54.711 --> 00:08:57.671
You can't say, "I've done this before."

00:08:57.671 --> 00:08:58.831
"I've sold this before."

00:08:58.831 --> 00:09:00.371
What you could say is, "I
truly believe I can do it

00:09:00.371 --> 00:09:03.231
"because of these things."

00:09:03.231 --> 00:09:05.492
But faking the funk

00:09:05.492 --> 00:09:08.031
has diminishing returns,

00:09:08.031 --> 00:09:09.931
puts you in a precarious spot,

00:09:09.931 --> 00:09:13.071
and when you define it as "lying" versus

00:09:13.071 --> 00:09:17.531
"a slight little embellishment"
or a little bit of hyperbole

00:09:17.531 --> 00:09:20.591
when you go to lying, I
think that has no place

00:09:20.591 --> 00:09:23.591
in the business world, or in life.

00:09:23.591 --> 00:09:26.051
And take it from somebody
who grew up a liar.

00:09:26.051 --> 00:09:29.131
It was very tough for me as
a kid to break that habit.

00:09:29.131 --> 00:09:31.652
This is one of the
disproportionate reasons

00:09:31.652 --> 00:09:34.931
I value my dad's fatherly advice.

00:09:34.931 --> 00:09:36.671
He really snapped me out of it.

00:09:36.671 --> 00:09:38.451
It's taken me a long time.

00:09:38.451 --> 00:09:41.071
I truly believe it's the reason
that most people struggle

00:09:41.071 --> 00:09:44.811
with consuming me at first,
and don't know how to take me,

00:09:44.811 --> 00:09:46.651
because I do think there's
a lot of bullshit DNA

00:09:46.651 --> 00:09:47.672
that I started with,

00:09:47.672 --> 00:09:50.651
but I chipped away, chipped away.

00:09:50.651 --> 00:09:53.571
And that's the scoop.

00:09:53.571 --> 00:09:55.605
No, no place.

00:09:55.605 --> 00:09:56.425
- Can you watch the video?

00:09:56.425 --> 00:09:57.025
